SELECT '78e7814f-6b8b-7982-8b5e-31e4393324cd', '18b51ec3-90ad-7234-b4e0-a261a0103efe', 'Primitives vs References', 'Primitives vs References', 'Primitives are copied by value; objects are shared by reference. Almost every “why did my data change?” bug comes from this.
Copying a primitive
let a = 10;
let b = a;     // b gets its own copy of the value
b = 20;
console.log(a, b);
10 20
Copying an object reference
const original = { score: 10 };
const alias = original;   // copies the *reference*, not the object
alias.score = 99;
console.log(original.score);
console.log(original === alias);
99
true
	Primitives	Objects (incl. arrays, functions)
Stored as	The value itself	A reference to a heap object
Assignment copies	The value	The reference
=== compares	Values	Identity (same object?)
Mutable?	No — immutable	Yes
Equality compares identity for objects
console.log("abc" === "abc");
console.log({ a: 1 } === { a: 1 });
console.log([1, 2] === [1, 2]);
const arr = [1, 2];
console.log(arr === arr);
true
false
false
true
Passing to functions: always by value (of the reference)
JavaScript is pass-by-value. For objects the value being passed is a reference — so a function can mutate the caller''s object, but reassigning the parameter never affects the caller.
function mutate(obj) {
  obj.touched = true;   // changes the shared object
}
function reassign(obj) {
  obj = { replaced: true };  // only rebinds the local parameter
}
const data = {};
mutate(data);
reassign(data);
console.log(data);
{ touched: true }
Primitives are immutable
const s = "hello";
const upper = s.toUpperCase();   // returns a NEW string
console.log(s, upper);
const t = "cat";
try {
  t[0] = "b";                    // strings can''t change in place
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.name + ": " + e.message);
}
console.log(t);
hello HELLO
TypeError: Cannot assign to read only property ''0'' of string ''cat''
cat
In strict mode (every ES module) the assignment throws; in a sloppy script it is silently ignored. Either way the string never changes.
Autoboxing
Methods on primitives work because of autoboxing: "hi".length briefly wraps the string in a String object, reads the property, and discards the wrapper.
Watch out
To copy an object, use spread ({...obj}, shallow) or structuredClone(obj) (deep) — see Immutability & Copying.
Is JavaScript pass-by-reference?
No. It is always pass-by-value; for objects the value is a reference (sometimes called call by sharing). Mutating through the parameter is visible to the caller, but reassigning the parameter is not.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Primitives vs References'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Primitives vs References'), 'B')
