INSERT INTO "notes"."NoteSearchDocument" ("noteId", "versionId", "title", "topics", "body", "document", "updatedAt")
SELECT 'cdefa73d-d1ed-718c-80aa-6f9ebe6f43a2', '2d65c806-87b2-7ed8-a40a-7790b662c8f1', 'CASE Expressions', 'CASE Expressions', 'CASE is SQL''s if/else. It is an expression — it returns a value and can be used anywhere a value can: SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, GROUP BY, inside aggregates.
Searched CASE
SELECT name, salary,
       CASE
           WHEN salary >= 120000 THEN ''Senior''
           WHEN salary >= 90000  THEN ''Mid''
           ELSE ''Junior''
       END AS band
FROM employees
ORDER BY salary DESC, emp_id;
name   | salary | band
-------+--------+-------
Asha   | 150000 | Senior
Ravi   | 120000 | Senior
Priya  | 110000 | Mid
Meera  | 95000  | Mid
Karan  | 95000  | Mid
Sara   | 85000  | Junior
Vikram | 80000  | Junior
Neha   | 72000  | Junior
Arjun  | 70000  | Junior
Dev    | 60000  | Junior
Conditions are tested top to bottom and the first true branch wins — so order the WHENs from most to least specific. Without ELSE, unmatched rows get NULL.
Simple CASE
SELECT order_id,
       CASE status
           WHEN ''delivered'' THEN ''Done''
           WHEN ''shipped''   THEN ''On the way''
           WHEN ''pending''   THEN ''Processing''
       END AS label
FROM orders
ORDER BY order_id;
order_id | label
---------+-----------
101      | Done
102      | Done
103      | Done
104      | On the way
105      | NULL
106      | Done
107      | Processing
108      | On the way
Watch out
A simple CASE x WHEN NULL THEN … never matches — it tests x = NULL. Use the searched form: CASE WHEN x IS NULL THEN ….
CASE inside aggregates: conditional counts
SELECT COUNT(*)                                           AS orders,
       SUM(CASE WHEN status = ''delivered'' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS delivered,
       SUM(CASE WHEN status = ''cancelled'' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS cancelled,
       COUNT(CASE WHEN order_date >= ''2024-03-01'' THEN 1 END) AS in_march
FROM orders;
orders | delivered | cancelled | in_march
-------+-----------+-----------+---------
8      | 4         | 1         | 3
This pattern — an aggregate over a CASE — is the basis of pivot queries (see Interview Prep). COUNT(CASE … THEN 1 END) works because unmatched rows produce NULL, which COUNT skips. PostgreSQL also offers COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE status = ''delivered'').
CASE in ORDER BY and UPDATE
-- different raise per band, in one statement
UPDATE employees
SET salary = salary + CASE WHEN salary < 80000 THEN 5000
                           WHEN salary < 100000 THEN 3000
                           ELSE 0 END;

SELECT name, salary FROM employees WHERE salary < 110000 ORDER BY salary, emp_id;
name   | salary
-------+-------
Dev    | 65000
Arjun  | 75000
Neha   | 77000
Vikram | 83000
Sara   | 88000
Meera  | 98000
Karan  | 98000
Key point
Short forms: IIF(cond, a, b) (SQL Server, SQLite), IF(cond, a, b) (MySQL), DECODE(x, v1, r1, …, default) (Oracle). CASE works everywhere.
Can you use CASE in a WHERE clause?
Yes — it is just an expression: WHERE CASE WHEN :role = ''admin'' THEN 1 WHEN owner_id = :me THEN 1 ELSE 0 END = 1. But plain AND/OR logic is usually clearer and friendlier to indexes.',
