SELECT '01c56c68-c0c5-78ea-a419-c38e96a8aa30', '5c15c2b0-5713-719e-ab21-5fbe4a7a94de', 'DISTINCT & Aliases', 'DISTINCT & Aliases', 'DISTINCT removes duplicate result rows; aliases give columns and tables short, readable names.
DISTINCT
SELECT DISTINCT category FROM products ORDER BY category;

SELECT DISTINCT city FROM customers ORDER BY city;       -- NULL counts as one value here
category
-----------
Electronics
Furniture
Stationery

city
---------
NULL
Bengaluru
Delhi
Mumbai
DISTINCT applies to the whole row, not to one column:
SELECT DISTINCT customer_id, status FROM orders ORDER BY customer_id, status;
customer_id | status
------------+----------
1           | delivered
1           | shipped
2           | delivered
3           | pending
3           | shipped
4           | cancelled
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS customers_who_ordered,
       COUNT(DISTINCT status)      AS statuses
FROM orders;
customers_who_ordered | statuses
----------------------+---------
4                     | 4
Watch out
Reaching for DISTINCT to hide duplicates caused by a join usually hides a bug (a missing join condition or a one-to-many join). Fix the join — or use EXISTS — instead.
-- PostgreSQL only: first row per group (by the ORDER BY)
SELECT DISTINCT ON (dept_id) dept_id, name, salary
FROM employees
ORDER BY dept_id, salary DESC;
Column aliases
SELECT name AS employee,
       salary * 12 AS "annual pay",       -- double quotes: spaces or reserved words
       hire_date joined                    -- AS is optional
FROM employees
WHERE emp_id <= 2
ORDER BY emp_id;
employee | annual pay | joined
---------+------------+-----------
Asha     | 1800000    | 2015-03-01
Ravi     | 1440000    | 2017-06-15
Table aliases
SELECT e.name, d.name AS department
FROM employees AS e
JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id
WHERE e.salary > 100000
ORDER BY e.emp_id;
name  | department
------+------------
Asha  | Engineering
Ravi  | Engineering
Priya | Sales
Key point
A table alias is required when the same table appears twice (self-join) and makes every multi-table query readable. Oracle does not accept AS before a table alias.
Watch out
A column alias cannot be used in WHERE of the same query (the SELECT list is evaluated after WHERE) — see the next topic. SQLite and MySQL relax this in places, which is not portable.
What is the difference between DISTINCT and GROUP BY?
SELECT DISTINCT a, b and SELECT a, b … GROUP BY a, b return the same rows and usually get the same plan. Use DISTINCT to de-duplicate; use GROUP BY when you also compute aggregates per group.',
