SELECT 'ecae93ff-c72a-7edc-b230-54f93fa77759', '95b5f434-264f-7dd2-9e14-9ecaa077033a', 'Joins & INNER JOIN', 'Joins & INNER JOIN', 'A join combines rows from two tables by matching a condition — usually a foreign key equal to a primary key. INNER JOIN keeps only the rows that match on both sides.
Why joins exist
Normalised tables store each fact once: an employee row holds dept_id = 1, not the text Engineering, Bengaluru. A join puts the pieces back together at query time.
Join	Returns
INNER JOIN	Only matching pairs
LEFT [OUTER] JOIN	All left rows + matches (NULLs where none)
RIGHT [OUTER] JOIN	All right rows + matches
FULL [OUTER] JOIN	All rows from both sides
CROSS JOIN	Every combination (Cartesian product)
Self join	A table joined to itself (hierarchies, comparisons)
INNER JOIN
SELECT e.name, d.name AS department, d.location
FROM employees e
INNER JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id
ORDER BY e.emp_id;
name   | department  | location
-------+-------------+----------
Asha   | Engineering | Bengaluru
Ravi   | Engineering | Bengaluru
Meera  | Engineering | Bengaluru
Karan  | Engineering | Bengaluru
Priya  | Sales       | Mumbai
Arjun  | Sales       | Mumbai
Neha   | Sales       | Mumbai
Vikram | HR          | Hyderabad
Sara   | HR          | Hyderabad
10 employees, but only 9 rows: Dev (dept_id NULL) matches no department, and Marketing matches no employee — an inner join drops both. JOIN alone means INNER JOIN.
How a join is evaluated (conceptually)
Pair up
Take every combination of a row from the left table with a row from the right table.
Keep matches
Keep only pairs for which the ON condition is true.
Continue
Further joins, WHERE, GROUP BY… work on this combined row set.
Key point
That is only the meaning. Physically the optimizer uses a nested-loop join (with an index on the inner side), a hash join (build a hash table on the smaller side) or a merge join (both inputs sorted on the key).
Joining several tables
-- what did each customer buy? (customers → orders → order_items → products)
SELECT c.name AS customer, o.order_id, p.name AS product, oi.quantity
FROM customers c
JOIN orders o       ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id   = o.order_id
JOIN products p     ON p.product_id  = oi.product_id
WHERE o.status = ''delivered''
ORDER BY o.order_id, p.name;
customer | order_id | product    | quantity
---------+----------+------------+---------
Ananya   | 101      | Headphones | 1
Ananya   | 101      | Laptop     | 1
Rahul    | 102      | Chair      | 2
Rahul    | 102      | Pen        | 10
Ananya   | 103      | Notebook   | 5
Ananya   | 103      | Pen        | 5
Rahul    | 106      | Laptop     | 1
ON vs USING vs NATURAL
-- USING: the column has the same name on both sides; it appears once in the output
SELECT order_id, customer_id, name, status
FROM orders JOIN customers USING (customer_id)
WHERE order_id <= 102
ORDER BY order_id;
order_id | customer_id | name   | status
---------+-------------+--------+----------
101      | 1           | Ananya | delivered
102      | 2           | Rahul  | delivered
Watch out
NATURAL JOIN joins on every column with the same name. employees NATURAL JOIN departments would match on both dept_id and name (employee name = department name) and return nothing. Avoid it — spell out the condition.
Join condition vs filter
For an inner join, a condition in ON or in WHERE gives the same result. Convention: relationships in ON, row filters in WHERE. For outer joins the placement changes the result — see the next topic.
Watch out
Forgetting the join condition (old comma syntax FROM a, b with no WHERE) creates a Cartesian product — 10 employees × 4 departments = 40 rows of nonsense. Explicit JOIN … ON makes the omission obvious.
What is the difference between JOIN and INNER JOIN?
None — INNER is optional. JOIN … ON … is an inner join.
Can you join on columns that are not keys?
Yes; any boolean condition works (ON a.city = b.city, ranges, inequalities). Joins on unindexed or non-unique columns can be slow or multiply rows, so know the cardinality of both sides.',
