SELECT '089d38c1-8413-7f74-acee-3431fe5376b0', 'eeba6bff-c0bd-7c72-aa39-35f9ad0c6833', 'LIMIT, OFFSET & Pagination', 'LIMIT, OFFSET & Pagination', 'LIMIT caps the number of rows returned; OFFSET skips rows first. Together they page through results — but deep offsets get slow.
Top-N
SELECT name, salary FROM employees
ORDER BY salary DESC, emp_id
LIMIT 3;
name  | salary
------+-------
Asha  | 150000
Ravi  | 120000
Priya | 110000
Watch out
LIMIT without ORDER BY returns an arbitrary 3 rows. And when the sort key has ties (Meera and Karan both 95000), add a unique tie-breaker (emp_id) so pages are stable.
Offset pagination
-- page 2, page size 3 → OFFSET = (page - 1) * size
SELECT emp_id, name FROM employees
ORDER BY emp_id
LIMIT 3 OFFSET 3;
emp_id | name
-------+------
4      | Karan
5      | Priya
6      | Arjun
Engine	Page 2 of size 3
SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL	LIMIT 3 OFFSET 3 (MySQL also LIMIT 3, 3 — offset first!)
SQL Server 2012+	ORDER BY emp_id OFFSET 3 ROWS FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY
Oracle 12c+ / standard	OFFSET 3 ROWS FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY
Old Oracle	WHERE ROWNUM <= n in a nested query
Keyset (seek) pagination
OFFSET 100000 still reads and throws away 100 000 rows, and rows inserted between requests shift pages (duplicates / gaps). Keyset pagination remembers the last key seen and seeks past it using the index.
-- the client got page 1 ending at emp_id = 3; next page:
SELECT emp_id, name FROM employees
WHERE emp_id > 3
ORDER BY emp_id
LIMIT 3;
emp_id | name
-------+------
4      | Karan
5      | Priya
6      | Arjun
-- sorted by a non-unique column: seek on (salary, emp_id) together; last row seen was Meera (95000, 3)
SELECT emp_id, name, salary FROM employees
WHERE (salary, emp_id) < (95000, 3)
ORDER BY salary DESC, emp_id DESC
LIMIT 3;
emp_id | name   | salary
-------+--------+-------
9      | Sara   | 85000
8      | Vikram | 80000
7      | Neha   | 72000
Offset pagination
Simple, allows jumping to page N. Cost grows with the offset; unstable when data changes.
Keyset pagination
Constant speed on any page, stable under inserts. No random page jumps — ideal for infinite scroll and APIs (?after=…).
Why is OFFSET pagination slow for large page numbers?
The database must produce and discard every row before the offset — OFFSET 1000000 reads a million rows to return 20. Keyset pagination (WHERE key > :last ORDER BY key LIMIT 20) jumps straight to the starting point through the index.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'LIMIT, OFFSET & Pagination'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'LIMIT, OFFSET & Pagination'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'LIMIT caps the number of rows returned; OFFSET skips rows first. Together they page through results — but deep offsets get slow.
