SELECT '54494534-1135-778a-a51e-8b13f18df716', '58fae47e-04ac-7fef-9b0a-9ee20bdf7396', 'Sorting with ORDER BY', 'Sorting with ORDER BY', 'Without ORDER BY, row order is undefined — it may look sorted today and change after an index or a version upgrade. If order matters, say so.
One or more sort keys
SELECT name, dept_id, salary
FROM employees
WHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY dept_id ASC, salary DESC, name;    -- ASC is the default
name   | dept_id | salary
-------+---------+-------
Asha   | 1       | 150000
Ravi   | 1       | 120000
Karan  | 1       | 95000
Meera  | 1       | 95000
Priya  | 2       | 110000
Neha   | 2       | 72000
Arjun  | 2       | 70000
Sara   | 3       | 85000
Vikram | 3       | 80000
Rows are sorted by the first key; ties are broken by the second, and so on. Meera and Karan tie on salary, so name decides.
Sort by expression, alias or position
SELECT name, salary / 12 AS monthly
FROM employees
WHERE dept_id = 2
ORDER BY monthly DESC;                      -- alias from the SELECT list

SELECT name, length(name) AS len
FROM employees
ORDER BY length(name) DESC, 1               -- expression, then column position 1
LIMIT 3;
name  | monthly
------+--------
Priya | 9166
Neha  | 6000
Arjun | 5833

name   | len
-------+----
Vikram | 6
Arjun  | 5
Karan  | 5
Watch out
ORDER BY 1 (column position) is handy interactively but fragile in code — reordering the select list changes the sort silently.
Where do NULLs go?
SQLite, MySQL and SQL Server treat NULL as smallest (first in ASC); PostgreSQL and Oracle treat it as largest (last in ASC). Be explicit with NULLS FIRST/NULLS LAST (PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQLite 3.30+).
SELECT name, email FROM employees WHERE emp_id >= 6 ORDER BY email;
SELECT name, email FROM employees WHERE emp_id >= 6 ORDER BY email NULLS LAST;
name   | email
-------+---------------
Neha   | NULL
Dev    | NULL
Arjun  | arjun@corp.in
Sara   | sara@corp.in
Vikram | vikram@corp.in

name   | email
-------+---------------
Arjun  | arjun@corp.in
Sara   | sara@corp.in
Vikram | vikram@corp.in
Neha   | NULL
Dev    | NULL
-- engines without NULLS LAST (MySQL, SQL Server): sort on "is null" first
SELECT name, email FROM employees WHERE emp_id >= 6
ORDER BY email IS NULL, email;
name   | email
-------+---------------
Arjun  | arjun@corp.in
Sara   | sara@corp.in
Vikram | vikram@corp.in
Neha   | NULL
Dev    | NULL
Custom order with CASE
SELECT order_id, status FROM orders
ORDER BY CASE status
             WHEN ''pending''   THEN 1
             WHEN ''shipped''   THEN 2
             WHEN ''delivered'' THEN 3
             ELSE 4
         END,
         order_id;
order_id | status
---------+----------
107      | pending
104      | shipped
108      | shipped
101      | delivered
102      | delivered
103      | delivered
106      | delivered
105      | cancelled
Can you ORDER BY a column that is not in the SELECT list?
Yes, in a plain query (SELECT name FROM employees ORDER BY salary). Not with SELECT DISTINCT or in some UNION cases, because the sort key must then be part of the output rows.',
