SELECT 'd446e058-9a62-7b33-8920-63488653739d', '58630266-e0d3-72f6-86db-c1c9cb229be0', 'LEFT, RIGHT & FULL OUTER JOIN', 'LEFT, RIGHT & FULL OUTER JOIN', 'Outer joins keep rows that have no match, filling the missing side with NULLs. Use them whenever “no related rows” is a valid answer.
LEFT JOIN
SELECT d.name AS department, e.name AS employee
FROM departments d
LEFT JOIN employees e ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id
ORDER BY d.dept_id, e.name;
department  | employee
------------+---------
Engineering | Asha
Engineering | Karan
Engineering | Meera
Engineering | Ravi
Sales       | Arjun
Sales       | Neha
Sales       | Priya
HR          | Sara
HR          | Vikram
Marketing   | NULL
Marketing appears with a NULL employee: every left row survives. Counting per department must count a column from the right side:
SELECT d.name, COUNT(e.emp_id) AS headcount, COUNT(*) AS wrong_count
FROM departments d
LEFT JOIN employees e ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id
GROUP BY d.dept_id, d.name
ORDER BY d.dept_id;
name        | headcount | wrong_count
------------+-----------+------------
Engineering | 4         | 4
Sales       | 3         | 3
HR          | 2         | 2
Marketing   | 0         | 1
Watch out
COUNT(*) counts the padded row, so Marketing shows 1. COUNT(e.emp_id) skips the NULL and correctly shows 0.
RIGHT and FULL OUTER JOIN
A RIGHT JOIN B is B LEFT JOIN A. FULL OUTER JOIN keeps unmatched rows from both sides. SQLite supports both since 3.39 (2022); MySQL has no FULL OUTER JOIN.
SELECT e.name AS employee, d.name AS department
FROM employees e
FULL OUTER JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id
ORDER BY d.dept_id NULLS LAST, e.emp_id;
employee | department
---------+------------
Asha     | Engineering
Ravi     | Engineering
Meera    | Engineering
Karan    | Engineering
Priya    | Sales
Arjun    | Sales
Neha     | Sales
Vikram   | HR
Sara     | HR
NULL     | Marketing
Dev      | NULL
-- MySQL workaround for FULL OUTER JOIN: LEFT JOIN ∪ the right-only rows
SELECT e.name AS employee, d.name AS department
FROM employees e LEFT JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id
UNION ALL
SELECT NULL, d.name
FROM departments d
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM employees e WHERE e.dept_id = d.dept_id)
ORDER BY department, employee;
employee | department
---------+------------
Dev      | NULL
Asha     | Engineering
Karan    | Engineering
Meera    | Engineering
Ravi     | Engineering
Sara     | HR
Vikram   | HR
NULL     | Marketing
Arjun    | Sales
Neha     | Sales
Priya    | Sales
ON vs WHERE in an outer join
List every customer with their delivered orders. The filter on the right table must go in ON — in WHERE it removes the NULL rows and silently turns the query into an inner join.
-- filter in ON: all 5 customers kept
SELECT c.name, o.order_id
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id AND o.status = ''delivered''
ORDER BY c.customer_id, o.order_id;
name   | order_id
-------+---------
Ananya | 101
Ananya | 103
Rahul  | 102
Rahul  | 106
Sneha  | NULL
Imran  | NULL
Kavya  | NULL
-- filter in WHERE: Sneha, Imran and Kavya disappear
SELECT c.name, o.order_id
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
WHERE o.status = ''delivered''
ORDER BY c.customer_id, o.order_id;
name   | order_id
-------+---------
Ananya | 101
Ananya | 103
Rahul  | 102
Rahul  | 106
Key point
Rule: conditions on the optional (right) side of a LEFT JOIN go in ON; conditions on the preserved (left) side go in WHERE. The exception is deliberately testing for NULL (WHERE o.order_id IS NULL), which finds non-matching rows — an anti-join.
What is the difference between LEFT JOIN and INNER JOIN?
INNER JOIN returns only rows with a match on both sides. LEFT JOIN returns every row of the left table, with the right table''s columns NULL where there is no match. The row count of a left join is always ≥ the left table''s count (more if one left row matches several right rows).
How do you emulate FULL OUTER JOIN in MySQL?
LEFT JOIN the two tables, then UNION ALL the rows of the right table that have no match (RIGHT JOIN … WHERE left.key IS NULL or NOT EXISTS). Plain UNION of a LEFT and a RIGHT join also works but de-duplicates, which can drop genuine duplicate rows.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'LEFT, RIGHT & FULL OUTER JOIN'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'LEFT, RIGHT & FULL OUTER JOIN'), 'B')
